<header><p class="st-kicker">other · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Alderney</h1><p class="st-meta">1204 CE – present (approx.) · capital Saint Anne</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The northernmost of the inhabited Channel Islands, a self-governing part of the Bailiwick of Guernsey with its own States and a long history as a fortress.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Alderney was part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy</a> and stayed loyal to the English crown when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> lost mainland Normandy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1204. Like the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/channel-islands">Channel Islands</a> it remained a possession of the crown in its role as Duke of Normandy, never part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The island was governed under Norman customary law, and from 1584 to 1825 held by hereditary governors. The British built a chain of Victorian forts and the breakwater at Braye. During the German occupation of 1940 to 1945 the population was evacuated and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazis</a> built labour camps there.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Alderney's residents returned in 1945. Since 1949 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bailiwick-of-guernsey">Guernsey</a> has run some of its public services, while Alderney keeps its own elected States within the Bailiwick of Guernsey, a British Crown Dependency.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/alderney.jpg" alt="Alderney" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Denelson83 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Alderney.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1204 CE – present (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saint Anne</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English; formerly Auregnais Norman French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guernsey">Guernsey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aurigny</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1615&amp;focus=alderney" aria-label="Alderney on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4629 447 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Alderney lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4949 703L4941 710"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4941 710h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4949 703h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4949 703h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Saint Anne. <a href="/atlas?year=1615&amp;focus=alderney">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

